# Line endings.
#
# Any shell script that reaches a Linux container must have LF endings. With
# Windows endings the shebang line ends in a carriage return, so the kernel
# looks for a program whose name ends in that character, does not find one, and
# the container exits 127 with one of the least obvious messages in the
# business -- it reports that "bash" (with an invisible character after it) does
# not exist.
#
# Globbed rather than named: this pinned startup.sh alone, so the next script
# anyone added would have been unprotected and the bug would have looked
# brand new.
*.sh text eol=lf

# The synthetic dataset and the SAP discovery snapshot are diffed and compared
# between sweeps. Without this a Windows checkout rewrites every line, and a
# real one-row change is buried in thousands of line-ending changes.
*.csv text eol=lf
*.xml text eol=lf
